+# IFRS S1/S2 Sustainability-Related Financial Disclosures
+
+# 1. General Requirements
+
+This section describes the basis on which the BankÔÇÖs sustainability-related financial disclosures have been prepared, including the reporting period, presentation currency, comparative information, connected information, and the key methodologies, estimates and judgements applied in preparing climate-related disclosures.
+
+### Basis of preparation and fair presentation
+
+The BankÔÇÖs sustainability-related financial disclosures are prepared to present fairly sustainability-related risks and opportunities that could reasonably be expected to affect the BankÔÇÖs prospects. Fair presentation is supported through disclosures that are intended to be complete, neutral and accurate, and that are prepared to be comparable, verifiable, timely and understandable.
+
+In preparing these disclosures, the Bank applies IFRS Sustainability Disclosure Standards. The disclosures are structured to enable users to understand connections:
+
+- **Between sustainability-related risks and opportunities** (for example, how acute physical risks to collateral can interact with credit risk outcomes, and how transition dynamics can affect sector exposures).
+- **Across the disclosure pillars** (governance, strategy, risk management, and metrics and targets), by aligning described processes (such as monitoring frequencies and board reporting cadence) with the metrics and scenario analysis outputs presented.
+
+### Reporting period, fiscal year-end and reporting year
+
+The reporting year for these sustainability-related financial disclosures is **2024**.
+
+The BankÔÇÖs fiscal year-end is **31 December 2024**.
+
+### Presentation currency
+
+The reporting currency used for sustainability-related financial disclosures is **euro (EUR)**. Where monetary amounts are presented (for example, climate-related capital expenditure and Tier 1 capital), they are presented in **EUR millions**.
+
+### Comparative information
+
+Comparative information is provided for **2022** and **2023** where available and relevant to understanding performance and trends.
+
+Selected climate-related financial and emissions metrics are presented across multiple years to support comparability, including:
+
+- Climate-related capital expenditure for **2022ÔÇô2024**.
+- Tier 1 capital for **2022ÔÇô2024**.
+- Scope 1 reporting years include **2022, 2023 and 2024**.
+
+### Connected information
+
+The Bank prepares sustainability-related financial disclosures in a manner intended to help users understand how information connects within the sustainability disclosures.
+
+**Connections between governance and risk monitoring.** Governance disclosures include the cadence of board and committee engagement and climate risk reporting, which provides context for how climate-related risks are overseen and monitored:
+
+- The Board has a size of **10** members, with **33.5%** climate expertise.
+- Climate topics represent **69.8%** of the Board agenda.
+- Climate risk reporting to the Board occurs **semi-annually**.
+- An ESG committee exists and meets **5** times per year.
+- Management committees referenced in governance information include the **Group Sustainability Committee**, **ESG Executive Committee**, and **Climate Risk Management Committee**.
+
+**Connections between risk identification and metrics.** The climate risk register includes risks described across physical and transition dimensions (for example, acute flood exposure affecting mortgage collateral; chronic heat impacts on agricultural counterparties; and transition dynamics such as rapid electrification affecting internal-combustion vehicle manufacturers and suppliers). These risk descriptions provide context for the BankÔÇÖs financed emissions metrics and scenario analysis.
+
+**Connections between scenario analysis and resilience statements.** Scenario methodology notes and resilience assessments are presented to explain how climate scenarios are applied to the banking book and how the Bank assesses portfolio resilience under orderly and disorderly transition pathways.
+
+### Methodologies
+
+This subsection summarises the principal methodologies used in preparing key climate-related disclosures, including risk rating, scenario analysis, and emissions accounting approaches.
+
+#### Climate risk rating methodology
+
+Climate risk ratings in the risk register are derived from a **5├ù5 risk matrix** based on the product of a **likelihood score** and a **severity score**. The rating bands are:
+
+- **Low:** score **< 3**
+- **Medium:** score **ÔëÑ 3**
+- **High:** score **ÔëÑ 8**
+- **Critical:** score **ÔëÑ 15**
+
+For example, the risk titled **ÔÇ£Physical risk ÔÇö mortgage book flood exposureÔÇØ** is rated **critical**, with a likelihood score of **4** and a severity score of **5**, consistent with a product score of **20**.
+
+Monitoring frequency is specified at risk level and includes **monthly**, **quarterly**, and **semi-annual** monitoring cadences. The risk register also indicates whether a risk has **changed since the prior period** for certain entries.
+
+#### Climate scenario analysis methodology
+
+The Bank applies climate scenarios to assess resilience of the banking book under different transition pathways.
+
+**NGFS v4 Net Zero 2050 (orderly transition).** The Bank applies the **NGFS v4 Net Zero 2050** scenario to the banking book using a **top-down sector-pathway approach**. Carbon price assumptions are taken from the **IEA Net Zero 2050 trajectory** and mapped to counterparties by **NACE sector**. Stranded asset estimates are derived from **sector-level fossil fuel capital expenditure exposure**. The analysis scope covers **all lending book exposures** across the jurisdictions **DE, FR, IT, NL and ES**.
+
+Resilience assessment under orderly transition scenarios indicates that the portfolio demonstrates **adequate resilience**, with transition risk losses remaining within **Pillar 2 capital buffer thresholds**. The assessment also notes that green loan growth and client engagement on transition plans are expected to progressively reduce high-carbon sector concentration over the medium term.
+
+**NGFS v4 Below 2┬░C (orderly transition).** The Bank also applies the **NGFS v4 Below 2┬░C (orderly)** scenario using NGFS integrated assessment model outputs aligned to a **1.8┬░C median temperature pathway**. The carbon price trajectory is sourced from the **REMIND-MAgPIE** model. Financial impact is assessed through **loan-level probability of default sensitivity** to carbon cost pass-through, differentiated by **sector carbon intensity tier**.
+
+Resilience assessment under these orderly transition scenarios is consistent with the conclusion of **adequate resilience**, with transition risk losses remaining within **Pillar 2 capital buffer thresholds**.
+
+**Disorderly transition scenarios.** Under disorderly transition scenarios, the BankÔÇÖs resilience assessment indicates that near-term resilience is acceptable, but that **medium-term capital consumption from stranded asset impairments is material**. The assessment identifies the BankÔÇÖs **sector exposure limits** and **active portfolio decarbonisation glide-path monitoring** as primary resilience mechanisms, and notes that additional capital buffers may be required post-2030 under the most adverse disorderly assumptions.
+
+#### Emissions accounting and market-based Scope 2 approach
+
+For Scope 2 emissions, the BankÔÇÖs **market-based Scope 2** figure is sourced directly from utility invoice data that already reflects market-based accounting. Grid emission factors are adjusted for **renewable energy certificates** and **power purchase agreements**. Renewable energy certificate registry data is used for disclosure narrative (certificate type, volume and registry) and is not subtracted again during preparation, avoiding double-counting.
+
+#### Financed emissions and methodological notes for sovereign bonds
+
+The Bank discloses financed emissions for loans and for the overall financed emissions metric in 2024. In applying financed emissions methodologies, the Bank notes that for **sovereign bonds**, issuer enterprise value including cash (EVIC) and issuer revenue are not applicable concepts; accordingly, these fields are not populated for sovereign bonds by design.
+
+### Key metrics, units of measure and comparability
+
+The Bank uses consistent units of measure for climate-related metrics to support comparability across periods.
+
+**Selected 2024 climate metrics** include:
+
+- **Financed emissions (2024):** 35,973,167.7 tCOÔéée
+- **Carbon intensity (2024):** 1,154.8 tCOÔéée per EUR million
+- **Climate-related capital expenditure (2024):** EUR 476.95 million
+- **Scope 1 emissions (2024):** 2,634.9 tCOÔéée (including fleet)
+- **Scope 2 emissions (2024):**
+  - Location-based: 6,963.5 tCOÔéée
+  - Market-based: 3,060.5 tCOÔéée
+- **Scope 3 business travel (2024):** 289.9 tCOÔéée
+- **Green loans (2024):** 10.71% of loans
+
+**Selected multi-year financial metrics** include:
+
+| Metric | 2022 | 2023 | 2024 |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Climate-related capital expenditure (EUR million) | 322.2 | 373.91 | 476.95 |
+| Tier 1 capital (EUR million) | 95,149.4 | 143,979.2 | 151,208.6 |
+
+### Estimates and judgement
+
+The preparation of sustainability-related financial disclosures involves the use of estimates and judgement, particularly where forward-looking analysis is required.
+
+- **Scenario analysis** incorporates judgement in selecting scenarios and translating macro pathways into sector and counterparty impacts. For example, the Bank maps carbon price trajectories to counterparties by NACE sector and assesses financial impacts through probability of default sensitivity to carbon cost pass-through by sector carbon intensity tier.
+- **Stranded asset estimation** under transition scenarios is derived from sector-level fossil fuel capital expenditure exposure, requiring judgement in translating sector-level indicators into portfolio-level implications.
+- **Risk rating** applies a defined matrix methodology based on likelihood and severity scoring; judgement is applied in assigning those underlying scores.
+
+### Data quality characteristics
+
+The Bank summarises the quality of emissions data used in its disclosures through a data-quality profile that distinguishes between audited, disclosed, estimated and modelled inputs. For the emissions data quality summary, the Bank reports the following composition:
+
+- **Estimated using economic data:** 47.5%
+- **CDP disclosure-based:** 35.5%
+- **Proxy model-based:** 16.3%
+- **Audited report-based:** 0.7%
+
+This profile is intended to support usersÔÇÖ understanding of the nature of inputs underpinning emissions-related metrics and the extent to which reported figures rely on estimation or modelling.
+
+### Value chain boundary and materiality mapping (selected elements)
+
+The Bank maps elements of its value chain and identifies nodes flagged as material. Within the BankÔÇÖs own operations, **ÔÇ£Corporate fleet & business travelÔÇØ** is identified as a material node. This is consistent with the inclusion of fleet in Scope 1 reporting and the disclosure of Scope 3 business travel emissions.
+
+### Consistency of data and assumptions
+
+Data and assumptions used in preparing sustainability-related financial disclosures are prepared to be consistent, to the extent possible, with corresponding data and assumptions used in preparing related financial statements. This includes the use of the BankÔÇÖs reporting currency (EUR) when currency is specified as a unit of measure and the presentation of financial metrics (such as climate-related capital expenditure and Tier 1 capital) in EUR millions.
+
+# 2. Governance
+
+This section describes how climate-related risks and opportunities are overseen and governed, including the roles of the Board and management-level committees, how climate matters are integrated into enterprise risk management, and how competence and remuneration are linked to climate-related performance.
+
+### Board oversight and agenda integration
+
+The Board provides oversight of climate-related risks and opportunities through its regular governance processes. The Board comprises **10 directors** and meets **four times per year** in full session.
+
+Climate-related matters are integrated into Board deliberations and are a recurring agenda item. During the period, climate-related topics were discussed across Board meetings, including **transition plan review**, **scenario analysis**, **review of climate-related disclosures (including TCFD review)**, **net zero progress**, **approval of carbon credits**, and **executive remuneration ESG KPIs**. Climate-related items represented **69.8%** of Board agenda coverage (as measured by the proportion of Board agenda items that included climate-related content).
+
+This agenda integration supports Board oversight of:
+
+- **Strategy and transition planning** (through transition plan review and scenario analysis);
+- **Monitoring of progress** (through net zero progress updates);
+- **Decisions on climate-related instruments** (through carbon credit approvals); and
+- **Incentives and accountability** (through review of executive remuneration ESG KPIs).
+
+### Climate risk reporting flow to the Board
+
+The Board is informed about climate-related risks and opportunities through a defined reporting cadence. Climate risk reporting is provided to the Board on a **semi-annual** basis. This reporting flow is designed to enable the Board to oversee climate-related risks and opportunities as part of its governance responsibilities and to support oversight of climate-related targets and progress monitoring.
+
+In addition to the semi-annual climate risk reporting cycle, climate-related topics are brought to the Board for consideration through agenda items that include transition planning, scenario analysis, climate disclosure review, and progress updates.
+
+### Management governance: committee structure and roles
+
+Management oversight of climate-related risks and opportunities is delegated to a management-level committee. The management committee responsible is the **Climate Risk Management Committee**.
+
